{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And whatever the man would call every living creature, that is its name.</b> They said in the Midrash: the Holy One, blessed be He, passed before him all the beasts and wild animals, and he gave them names, saying of this one, 'it is fitting to call it אריה (lion),' and of this one, 'it is fitting to call it חמור (donkey),' and of this one, 'סוס (horse),' and so for each and every thing. And behold, by this his great wisdom was made known, as he was in the image of Elohim and the handiwork of HaShem." "<b>And it appears</b> to me [to explain] the meaning of this Midrash: that the man understood, through his wisdom and intellect, the nature of every beast and wild animal, and he called each and every one a name patterned after the nature and quality he recognized in it, and the letters he combined in their names were all according to their natures and qualities. And behold, the names were intelligible, as you would say: he recognized by his wisdom the nature of the lion, that it is mighty, great, and king among the wild animals, to the point that the prophets compared HaShem, blessed be He, to it, as it says (Hosea 11): ״אחרי ה׳ ילכו כאריה ישאג״. And he derived its name אריה, because the letters of אריה are spiritual: for the אל״ף and the ה״א and the יו״ד are letters of HaShem, and the רי״ש means רוח (spirit/wind); if so, the very name of אריה explains its matter. And similarly the eagle (נשר): he recognized by his wisdom that the eagle is king among birds, and its flight is higher than every bird, as is known from its ways — for when it completes ten years it raises its flight exceedingly until it draws near the sphere of fire, and because of the intense heat it flings and casts itself into the sea, and it is plucked, and afterward it renews itself and its limbs grow back as at first; and so is its way every ten years, up to a hundred years, and in the hundredth year it draws nearer than ever to the sphere of fire and raises its flight as is its way, and falls into the sea and dies; and so said Rav Saadia Gaon, of blessed memory. Therefore the man knew this matter through his wisdom and called its name נשר, for the שי״ן is אש (fire), and the רי״ש is רוח (wind/spirit), and the נו״ן is from the language of נפילה (falling); and behold, its name explains its way and nature and what its end is. And this is what it says: 'of this one it is fitting to call it נשר.' And the expression 'it is fitting' for each and every one is as if to say 'fitting and worthy.' And similarly with the name חמור (donkey): he recognized by his wisdom that it is foolish and stupid and heavier than all the beasts, drawn after desires; therefore he called its name חמור, from the word חומר (matter/material). And furthermore, the way of the donkey is to carry loads of grain, which are also measures, from the word חומר, a measure of barley. And similarly with the name סוס (horse): he recognized by his wisdom that the horse is swift of movement and rejoices to go to battle, and he derived its name סוס from the word שישה (six — שש), for the סמ״ך and שי״ן interchange, and it is written (Job 39:25): ״בדי שופר יאמר האח ומרחוק יריח מלחמה״. And so he gave names to each and every thing. And the Midrash concludes: after he had given names to all of them, He said to him, 'And what is My name?' He said to Him, 'YHWH.' He said to him, 'Why?' — 'Because You are Lord (אדון) over all creatures'; that is what is written (Isaiah 42:8): ״אני ה׳ הוא שמי״ — this is My name, which the first man called Me. End quote. And if you ask, where did he call His name יו״ד ה״א — if you remove the חי״ת you will find it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :register {:flags {1 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:32.248834297Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}